# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_JOB_CREATE */
int64_t job_create(uint32_t parent, uint64_t options, uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_JOB_SET_POLICY */
int64_t job_set_policy(uint32_t job, uint32_t options, uint32_t topic,
                       const croi_policy_basic_t *policy, uint64_t count);
/* CROI_SYS_JOB_SET_CPU_RESERVATION */
int64_t job_set_cpu_reservation(uint32_t job, uint32_t cpu, uint64_t cpu_mask);
```

# DESCRIPTION

Jobs form a tree.
A job holds processes and child jobs; the root job is the ancestor of all.
A job's policy limits what its processes may do, and its processes and child jobs end with it (task_kill(2)).
The handle of a job asserts `CROI_SIGNAL_JOB_NO_JOBS` while it has no child job and `CROI_SIGNAL_JOB_NO_PROCESSES` while it has no process.

## job_create

`job_create` makes a child job of <parent>, which needs MANAGE_JOB, and writes its handle to <out>.
<options> must be 0.
The child starts with its parent's policy and CPU reservation.
Its handle has the basic rights and READ, WRITE, GET_PROPERTY, SET_PROPERTY, GET_POLICY, SET_POLICY, ENUMERATE, DESTROY, SIGNAL, MANAGE_JOB, MANAGE_PROCESS and MANAGE_THREAD.
A parent that has been killed is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`.

## job_set_policy

`job_set_policy` sets the action for each of up to 16 conditions, read from <policy>, an array of

```c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t condition;
  uint32_t policy;
} croi_policy_basic_t;
```

The job needs SET_POLICY, <topic> must be 0 and <count> at most 16 (`CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`).
It is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` once the job has a child, so a policy is set before anything is created under it.
<options> is `CROI_JOB_POL_RELATIVE`, which skips a condition the job already restricts differently, or `CROI_JOB_POL_ABSOLUTE`, which fails with `CROI_ERR_ALREADY_EXISTS` for one.
A <condition> is:

| Condition | Value | Applies to |
| `CROI_POL_BAD_HANDLE` | 0 | a call given a handle that is not in the table |
| `CROI_POL_NEW_ANY` | 3 | sets all of the five NEW conditions below |
| `CROI_POL_NEW_VMO` | 4 | vmo_create, vmo_create_physical, vmo_create_contiguous, pager_create_vmo |
| `CROI_POL_NEW_CHANNEL` | 5 | channel_create, task_create_exception_channel |
| `CROI_POL_NEW_PORT` | 8 | port_create |
| `CROI_POL_NEW_PROCESS` | 12 | process_create |
| `CROI_POL_NEW_PAGER` | 14 | pager_create |

and the <policy> is an action:

| Action | Value | Does |
| `CROI_POL_ACTION_ALLOW` | 0 | nothing |
| `CROI_POL_ACTION_DENY` | 1 | the call fails with `CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED` |
| `CROI_POL_ACTION_ALLOW_EXCEPTION` | 2 | raises a policy exception in the caller and lets the call go on |
| `CROI_POL_ACTION_DENY_EXCEPTION` | 3 | raises the exception and fails the call |
| `CROI_POL_ACTION_KILL` | 4 | kills the process, with return code `CROI_TASK_RETCODE_POLICY_KILL` |

The policy applies to the calls made by the processes of the job.
A condition or action not listed is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
A policy exception is `CROI_EXCP_POLICY_ERROR` (exception(2)); if no handler handles it the process is killed.
With `CROI_POL_BAD_HANDLE` the call that used the bad handle still returns `CROI_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`.

## job_set_cpu_reservation

`job_set_cpu_reservation` reserves the CPUs in <cpu_mask> (bit n is CPU n) for the job: they run only threads of this job and its descendants, and no other threads, timers or routed interrupts.
It needs SET_POLICY on <job> and <cpu> a resource(2) that grants the CPU system resource.
It is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` while the job has children or when an ancestor holds a reservation.
The boot CPU cannot be reserved (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`); a CPU another job holds is `CROI_ERR_ALREADY_BOUND`; a CPU with deadline reservations admitted for others is `CROI_ERR_NO_RESOURCES`.
A <cpu_mask> of 0 releases the reservation.
Destroying the job releases it too.

